WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.390
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:10.500
If we study the laws of fluid motion and implement
them in a computer program, we can create

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.679
and enjoy these beautiful fluid simulations.

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:18.880
And not only that, but today, with the amazing
progress in computer graphics research, we

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:25.100
can even enrich our physics simulations with
anisotropic damage and elasticity.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.600
So what does that mean exactly?

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.310
This means that we can simulate more extreme
topological changes in these virtual objects.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:36.879
This leads to better material separation when
the damage happens.

00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:42.809
So it appears that today we can do a great
deal, but these techniques are pretty complex,

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:46.890
and take quite a while to compute, and they
typically run on your processor.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:53.199
That’s a pity, because many powerful consumer
computers also have a graphics card, and if

00:00:53.199 --> 00:00:57.750
we could restate some of these algorithms
to be able to run them on those, they would

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.809
run significantly faster.

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:02.739
So, do we have any hope for that?

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:08.290
Well, today’s paper promises a new particle
data structure that is better suited for number

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:14.420
crunching on the graphics card, and is, hence,
much faster than its predecessors.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.750
As a result, this runs the Material Point
Method, the algorithm that is capable of simulating

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.740
these wondrous things you are seeing here,
not only on your graphics card, but the work

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:30.200
on one problem can be also be distributed
between many-many graphics cards.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:36.200
This means that we get crushing concrete,
falling soil, candy bowls, sand armadillos,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.920
oh my!

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:38.920
You name it.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.400
And all this, much faster than before.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.020
Now, since these are some devilishly detailed
simulations, please do not expect several

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:52.290
frames per second kind of performance, we
are still in the seconds per frame region,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.660
but we are not that far away.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:02:00.680
For instance, hold on to your papers because
this candy bowl example contains nearly 23

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:07.410
million particles, and despite that, it runs
in about 4 seconds per frame on a system equipped

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.659
with four graphics cards.

00:02:09.659 --> 00:02:11.439
Four seconds per frame!

00:02:11.439 --> 00:02:12.500
My goodness!

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.879
If somebody told this to me today without
showing me this paper, I would have not believed

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:18.879
a word of it.

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:20.480
But there is more.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.819
You know what, let’s double the number of
particles and pull up this dam break scene.

00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:32.129
What you see here is 48 million particles
that runs in 15 seconds per frame.

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:34.620
Let’s do even more.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:40.629
These sand armadillos contain a total of 55
million particles, and take about 30 seconds

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:41.829
per frame.

00:02:41.829 --> 00:02:46.780
And in return, look at that beautiful mixture
of the two sand materials.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:51.409
And with half a minute per frame, that’s
a great deal, I’ll take this any day of

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:52.810
the week.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.629
And if we wish to simulate crushing this piece
of concrete with a hydraulic press, that will

00:02:57.629 --> 00:03:01.300
take nearly a hundred million particles.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.330
Just look at that footage.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.519
This is an obscene amount of detail.

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:10.380
And the price to be paid for this is nearly
four minutes of simulation time for every

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.529
frame that you see on the screen here.

00:03:13.529 --> 00:03:14.599
Four minutes you say?

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:18.129
Hmm, that’s a little more than expected.

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:19.279
Why is that?

00:03:19.279 --> 00:03:23.879
We had several seconds per frame for the others,
not minutes per frame.

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:30.099
Well, it is because the particle count matters
a great deal, but that’s not the only consideration

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:31.569
for such a simulation.

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:37.219
For instance, here, you see with delta t something
that we call time step size.

00:03:37.219 --> 00:03:42.529
The smaller this number is, the tinier the
time steps with which we can advance the simulation

00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:47.599
when computing every interaction, and hence,
the more steps there are to compute.

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:53.260
In simpler words, generally, time step size
is also an important factor in the computation

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.319
time, and the smaller this is, the slower
the simulation will be.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:02.040
As you see, we have to simulate 5 times more
time steps to make sure that we don’t miss

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.670
any particle interactions, and hence, this
takes much longer.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:12.610
Now, this one appears to be perhaps the simplest
simulation of the bunch, isn’t it?

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:14.389
No, no, no.

00:04:14.389 --> 00:04:15.569
Quite the opposite!

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:20.350
If you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now squeeze that paper, because, and

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:21.739
watch carefully.

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:23.610
They wiggle.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:25.479
They… wiggle!

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:27.090
Why do they wiggle?

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:33.360
These are just around 6 thousand bombs, which
is not a lot, however, wait a minute…each

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:39.780
bomb is a collection of particles, giving
us a total of not a measly 6 thousand, but,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:46.580
a whopping 134 million particle simulation,
and hence, we may think that it’s nearly

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:50.169
impossible to perform in a reasonable amount
of time.

00:04:50.169 --> 00:04:54.699
The time steps are not that far apart for
this one, so we can do it in less than one

00:04:54.699 --> 00:04:56.389
minute per frame.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:01.970
This was nearly impossible when I started
my PhD, and today, less than a minute for

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:02.970
one frame.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:07.130
It truly feels like we are living in a science
fiction world.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:08.130
What a time to be alive!

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:13.730
I also couldn’t resist creating a slow-motion
version of some of these videos, so if this

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:18.210
is something that you wish to see, make sure
to visit our Instagram page in the video description

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.060
for more.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:58.310
Thanks for watching and for 
your generous support, and I'll see you next

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:14.840
time!

